{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":5,"timestamp":1779765848,"nonce":"MyQjWtanIziaLLxP6zC4dzMLlmc=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOSn4-Gc8AAAABDXobSM8AAAAGCq4KcA"},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779838525,"nonce":"bkCNj32T4BwdlWvpot+jSRx2aTU=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABDzI0MA","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/5#issuecomment-4549915696"},"message":"Won't do — doesn't make sense.\n\nEach Cloudspace is its own Kubernetes cluster, and Karpenter's design assumes a single cluster (scheduler watches pods in the cluster it runs in; the cloud provider creates nodes that join that same cluster). Managing pools in Cloudspace B from a Karpenter running in Cloudspace A would create nodes that join B, not A — the bookkeeping is broken from the start.\n\nThe correct pattern for multiple Cloudspaces is one Karpenter install per Cloudspace, which is already how the provider works. This was a mis-framing on my part when the issue was opened.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1779838525,"nonce":"K1IFzOsi3FhrikgnRvoSRgGUBAg=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADOSn4-Gc8AAAABDXobSM8AAAAGDS5lYg"},"status":2}]}